@*
    Read view on the left, the markdown that produced it on the right.

    Chrome, not a component: it frames an example and has no behaviour of its
    own. Every markdown story wants this same pairing, and repeating the two
    columns in each one is how the source and the rendering drift apart — one
    story showing a sample it no longer renders is worse than no sample.

    The source is a `pre`, so it is read-only by construction rather than by a
    flag someone has to remember to set. ParseDocument, not Parse: this chapter
    is about markdown, where a `##` is a heading.
*@

<div class="sb-columns">
    <div class="sb-stack">
        <p class="sb-label">Read view</p>
        <div data-testid="@TestId">
            <MarkdownView Blocks="Blocks" />
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sb-stack">
        <p class="sb-label">Source</p>
        <pre class="sb-story__code" data-testid="@SourceTestId"><code>@Source.Trim()</code></pre>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The markdown, shown verbatim and rendered beside itself.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Source { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    private string? SourceTestId => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-source";

    // Parsed when the source changes rather than on every render. These are
    // constants in practice, so this parses once.
    private IReadOnlyList<MdBlock> Blocks => _blocks ??= MarkdownPreview.ParseDocument(Source);

    private IReadOnlyList<MdBlock>? _blocks;

    protected override void OnParametersSet() => _blocks = null;
}
